# Launcher Stability

The two macOS launcher stubs at the project root MUST stay stable across
agent edits:

- `run_web.command`
- `run_desktop.command`

Each stub is a ~10-line shell file that only computes its own directory
and `exec`s `/bin/zsh` on the real launcher under `scripts/`. The real
launcher logic lives in:

- `scripts/launcher-web.sh`
- `scripts/launcher-desktop.sh`

## Why the stubs must not be edited from Cursor

When Cursor (or any other GUI app) writes a file, macOS attaches
kernel-managed extended attributes (`com.apple.provenance`, and on some
versions `com.apple.macl`). On macOS Sequoia/Tahoe, `AppleSystemPolicy`
(ASP) then refuses to `execve()` that file when it is launched from
Finder/Terminal.app, which lack the "App Management" entitlement. The
process is SIGKILL'd in the kernel before the script's first `echo`, so
no launcher log is created and the failure looks like a silent crash.
The unified system log shows entries such as:

  kernel: (AppleSystemPolicy) ASP: Security policy would not allow
  process: <PID>, /path/to/run_web.command

Those xattrs are kernel-managed and cannot be removed by `xattr -d` or
`xattr -c` from inside Cursor's process tree, because the kernel
immediately re-tags any file Cursor touches.

The split-stub design works around this by ensuring the file Finder
double-clicks (`*.command`) is never modified by Cursor again, so it
never receives a provenance tag. The real logic file is invoked as
`/bin/zsh ./scripts/launcher-X.sh`, which only `execve`s `/bin/zsh`
(Apple-signed, always allowed); the script argument is read as data,
not executed, so ASP never inspects it.

## Rules for AI agents

1. NEVER call `Write`, `StrReplace`, `EditNotebook`, or any other
   content-modifying tool on `run_web.command` or `run_desktop.command`.
   Treat them as read-only.
2. To change launcher behavior, edit `scripts/launcher-web.sh` or
   `scripts/launcher-desktop.sh` instead. The stubs do not need to
   change for any logic update.
3. If the stub itself genuinely must change (e.g., renaming the real
   script path), do not edit it directly. Tell the human exactly what
   new contents to paste and ask them to apply the edit from their own
   Terminal.app, then run `bash scripts/bless-launchers.sh` to strip
   any leftover provenance xattr.
4. `scripts/bless-launchers.sh` is the one-shot human repair tool. Keep
   it idempotent. Do not invoke it from Cursor's terminal yourself; the
   bless only takes effect when run from the human's Terminal.app
   because Cursor's process tree re-tags any file it touches.
5. The real launcher scripts (`scripts/launcher-*.sh`) may be edited
   freely. They are read as zsh script arguments and never `execve`'d
   directly, so ASP never inspects them.

## Diagnostic shortcut

If the human reports "double-click does nothing" or "Killed: 9" with no
launcher log file under `data/logs/`, run from their Terminal.app:

  /usr/bin/log show --last 5m \
    --predicate 'eventMessage CONTAINS "AppleSystemPolicy"' \
    --info

ASP rejections look like the `Security policy would not allow process`
line cited above. The fix is `bash scripts/bless-launchers.sh` from
their Terminal, not from Cursor's terminal.
